@title Independent Ordinary Project Permissions
@group developer

Independent ordinary project membership and permission semantics.

This change targets `test/phorge-permission`, based on
`4d9b8b40389c5d24f76ab57513349aca338731d5`. It does not add a `project.manage`
capability, a creator override, a migration, or a production configuration.

## Contract

| Behavior | Ordinary parent / child | Milestone |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Members | Own direct members, regardless of children | Immediate parent's members |
| View / edit / join policies | Own native policies | Immediate parent's policies |
| Creating a child | No copied or moved members | Membership inherited, not copied |
| Membership editing | Native editor and join/leave checks | Edit the parent instead |
| Hierarchy | Organizational links, tags, board and ancestor searches retained | Original milestone organization retained |

Native automatic rules still apply: a project's own members can view it, and
its own editors can join it. Native edit, application, Space, CSRF and transaction
checks are retained. Ordinary projects have the controlled handoff described
below instead of requiring the actor to retain access. Other object types and
milestones retain native self-lockout checks. Project policy selection is unchanged.
The same membership can be deliberately assigned to several projects, and a
policy can explicitly reference another project's members; those are explicit
grants, not hierarchy inheritance.

Existing ancestor watch subscriptions and organizational task searches are not
removed. Watching does not grant membership or object access; notification
recipient checks still use the target object's view policy. The existing
`ITEM_MANAGE` navigation key is retained; it is not a new management capability.

Creating an ordinary child still requires view and edit access to the parent.
The initial Space default is unchanged. A child's own permissive policy is no
longer constrained by a hidden ordinary parent. A milestone still requires view
access to its immediate parent. Ancestor names, PHIDs and links are filtered in
breadcrumbs and Conduit output. Hierarchy search constraints accept only visible
parent/ancestor projects. `fields.parent` is null for a hidden parent, just as for
a root; the ancestors attachment contains only visible entries. Depth and
ordinary organizational search semantics are retained, so this does not promise
to hide the existence of hierarchy levels or redact user-authored text.

## Implementation

- Edge 13 (`PhabricatorProjectProjectHasMemberEdgeType`) and its inverse edge 14
  remain the direct membership source of truth.
- Edge 60 (`PhabricatorProjectMaterializedMemberEdgeType`) indexes own members
  for ordinary projects and direct parent members for milestones.
- Membership transactions reindex the changed project and its direct milestones.
  They do not change ordinary ancestors, siblings or descendants.
- `hasSubprojects` and `hasMilestones` remain structural database flags and do
  not disable ordinary membership editing. Creation and destruction still update
  structural flags without using an actor's view policy to count children.
- Sparse, full and ancestor-member project queries use the same own membership
  data. Object-member policies use direct inverse edges; milestone policies
  resolve to their immediate parent, including transaction hints.
- Raw ancestor objects remain available for internal hierarchy operations.
  User-facing consumers must apply the viewer's policy before returning them.

### Search constraints and hidden intermediate projects

For G (visible) -> P (hidden) -> C (visible), a task search containing G, P and
C must not drop P as redundant before checking P's visibility. Otherwise the
result differs from substituting an unrelated hidden project for P, exposing
the hidden relationship. `PhabricatorProjectLogicalAncestorDatasource` now
loads every original plain project condition with the actual viewer before
expanding descendants or simplifying conditions. A missing or inaccessible
condition yields `OPERATOR_EMPTY`, not an empty list of constraints. Maps only
include ancestors which were original, authorized conditions. Existing
downstream edge-constraint and task-object policy checks remain in place.

The Web field's slug parser also previously dropped hidden or unknown slugs
before they reached this check. `PhabricatorProjectSearchField` now uses the
native, viewer-filtered slug map and preserves unresolved inputs for rejection.
Visible slug normalization and deduplication remain supported. A URL or saved
query with an invalid/inaccessible project must now return no results rather
than silently search without that condition. This is an intentional change.

The shared paths reviewed are:

- `PhabricatorProjectsSearchEngineExtension`: project-tag fields in supported
  application searches, including Web Maniphest and `maniphest.search`, use
  the same datasource. The Conduit PHID resolver does not prove visibility;
  the datasource and downstream query must still validate resolved inputs.
- `PhabricatorApplicationSearchEngine::setQueryProjects()`: this retained
  helper uses the same datasource; no callers of the helper were found in the
  current tree. There is no separate copy of the redundant-ancestor algorithm.
- `PhabricatorWorkboardViewState`: saved task queries use the shared engine;
  additional board constraints use visible descendants and do not discard
  original search conditions. `ManiphestTaskColumnTransaction` constructs
  internal board-layout constraints without this simplification. Neither
  path's permissions or transaction behavior were relaxed.
- `any()` and `not()` produce constraint objects, which are not simplified by
  the plain-project loop. The existing downstream visibility check rejects
  hidden operands. `only()` does not remove an empty constraint.

This is a review of the shared project-search paths, not a whole-application
security audit. Visible G/C organizational relationships may still be searched;
P's existence, name or relation is not disclosed by accepting a hidden input.
The fix does not require viewing P to view or directly search public C.

## Controlled ordinary-project handoff

Ordinary child creation links directly to the native form. The legacy warning
route rechecks parent visibility, edit access and the application creation
capability before redirecting. Milestone creation is unchanged.

Only `PhabricatorProjectTransactionEditor` replaces the native policy
self-retention validation. It still uses the common transaction editor's
original capability checks. `PhabricatorProjectHandoff` validates the combined
final policies, Space and normalized direct-member transaction before saving
the project or writing its transactions/edges. It does not grant a creator
override or introduce a management capability.

When the actor loses view or edit access, at least one approved, enabled human
account must pass the native `PhabricatorPolicyFilter` for both capabilities
on the candidate final state, and must be allowed to use the Projects
application. Membership uses the final direct-member list and
a separate native transaction hint; original authorization uses the original
list. Project-member automatic view access and Space restrictions still apply.
The actor's original authorization is rechecked from locked dependencies.
Noneditor self-join/leave retains native authorization, while removing the last
editing member is rejected. Unrelated edits to legacy projects are not a data
migration or an automatic repair of pre-existing policy problems.

The bounded handoff proof supports global constants, explicit users,
`obj.project.members`, and custom policies composed of Users, Administrators,
Global and ProjectMembers rules. It enumerates final members and explicit users
as recipients. It deliberately rejects a handoff relying on named policies,
other projects' membership, time-dependent or unknown rules, or a global rule
with no provable candidate. These policies remain usable on the native
self-retaining path. Rejection does not relax access checks; use an explicit
approved recipient for a transfer. Original authorization and Space policies
must also use provable dependency types for the handoff path.

Application-level configuration is checked for the request but is not a
transactional project setting. Do not change application access settings or
database routing concurrently with permission handoffs.

Existing project and new-child parent rows are locked. Relevant user, custom
policy and Space rows are locked through the **same project transaction
connection**, using cross-schema reads. All Spaces are locked to stabilize the
default-Space decision. This implementation requires the relevant schemas on
one MySQL server with transactional tables and suitable schema permissions;
sharded or differently routed database deployments are not certified. A lock
failure or deadlock aborts the transaction and must be retried as a new request.
It proves a recipient at commit time, not perpetual access after a later
administrator disables that account or changes a Space. External direct SQL
or low-level edge writes which bypass transaction editors are outside this
contract and must not run concurrently with application writes.

Web handoffs return a native confirmation dialog before writes. Its signed,
15-minute token binds the actor/session, original policies and members, final
state, dependency snapshots and complete form payload. Confirmation goes
through all checks again; missing, forged, changed or expired confirmation
never authorizes a write. Cancel does not save (a full-page cancel returns to
the form and may discard unsaved text). If the actor loses view access, success
shows only a generic saved message and a link to the project list. Conduit and
internal editors use the same proof without an interactive dialog. The generic
PolicyFilter and other applications' error handling are unchanged.

The handoff suite temporarily commits fixtures so independent DB connections
can test real lock contention. It has an explicit randomized
`phabricator_unittest_*` namespace guard and must only run on isolated storage.
It covers creation for another user, member and explicit custom policies,
Web confirmation/forgery/replay, loss of view, rejected mixed writes, inactive
recipients, stale editors, concurrent recipient disable and Space revocation,
native parent/application gates, and milestones.

## Historical data: deployment gate

**Do not deploy over an existing database or run a global reindex without an
approved membership and policy reconciliation plan.** There is no automatic
migration in this branch.

The old first-child operation copied direct parent edges to the child. It did
not record whether an edge was copied or intentionally added. Old parent direct
edges may therefore be stale, while edge 60 may contain a union of descendant
members. Rebuilding from edge 13 may remove previously effective users or revive
stale direct members. Converting edge 60 to edge 13 can grant new editing rights
through `obj.project.members`. Neither is a safe universal migration rule.

Additionally, a child with a broad view policy may become visible when the
parent-view requirement is removed, even when policy columns are unchanged.
Task, Wiki and file policies that reference project membership need review.
Project tags alone do not establish those objects' access policies.

Before a separately authorized deployment:

1. Take a consistent database backup and record the application image/version.
2. Produce a read-only manifest of all ordinary parent/child projects, direct
   edges (13/14), materialized edges (60), milestones, Spaces and all three
   policies. Record users who gain or lose access under the proposed model.
3. Ask project owners to approve explicit member lists and any necessary policy
   changes, including dependencies from tasks, documents and files. Ensure each
   project retains an intended effective editor; ownership is not an override.
4. Restore a copy into an isolated database. Apply the approved manifest there,
   rebuild derived indexes there and repeat the matrix below. Retain before/after
   manifests, direct-edge provenance limits and rollback evidence.
5. Only after separate approval, schedule a coordinated application/data cutover.
   Do not mix old and new membership writers. Queue workers and CLI index jobs
   must use the same model. Even a routine membership edit or background reindex
   can replace a legacy edge-60 union, so postponing an explicit reindex does not
   make an unreconciled database safe.

Rollback before data conversion is a code rollback. After new membership writes
or conversion, restoring the old image alone does not restore the old model:
reconcile against the approved manifest and rebuild appropriate indexes with a
reviewed rollback procedure. Do not automatically restore an old production
snapshot over later business writes.

## Isolated regression testing

Run on an isolated installation only, with a dedicated MySQL instance. Native
`PhabricatorTestCase` creates randomized `phabricator_unittest_*` namespaces,
uses fixture transactions and removes the test namespace afterwards. It still
needs database creation privileges and must never point at production.

Configure a separate Gorge file service for this installation before running
the suite: user-avatar fixtures write files through `gorge.file.uri` and
`gorge.file.token`. The historical local-disk engine is read-only, so setting
`storage.local-disk.path` alone is insufficient. Set `policy.allow-public` to
true in this isolated test configuration; the native named-policy cycle test
expects anonymous access to public policies. Keep these settings and test
credentials outside the source checkout. Neither service needs a host port.
The extended suite also needs the bundled runtime's prepared XHPAST and
PHP-Parser dependencies. These ignored build artifacts are absent from a fresh
Git checkout; use a matching prepared test image or build them in the disposable
test environment before running class-map and translation validation.

```sh
php bin/unit --no-coverage \
  src/applications/project/__tests__/PhabricatorProjectHandoffTestCase.php \
  src/applications/project/__tests__/PhabricatorProjectCoreTestCase.php \
  src/applications/policy/__tests__/PhabricatorPolicyTestCase.php \
  src/applications/policy/__tests__/PhabricatorPolicyDataTestCase.php
```

Regression coverage includes first/subsequent child creation, multi-level and
sibling membership isolation, ordinary parent member editing, repeated Conduit
changes, sparse/full/ancestor queries, materialized member searches, hidden
ancestors in full rendered pages and API responses, independent view/edit/join
policies, milestone inheritance and revocation, mixed transaction rejection,
Conduit creation, dependent task policies, and simulated legacy index divergence.
The previous child-edit form/transaction/API tests and structural destruction,
tag ancestry and board tests are retained with updated membership expectations.

For broader checks, use `src/applications/project/` and `src/applications/policy/`.
Generic library-map and translation tests need a writable disposable source
copy; never make the production checkout writable merely to satisfy these tests.

An in-process controller test is not a real browser or live gateway acceptance
test. Before any release, test Alice/Bob/unauthorized accounts in separate browser
profiles in a staging deployment; cover project creation, parent/child details,
member management, policy selectors, hidden ancestors, milestone operations,
and tasks/files with explicit membership-based policies. No production acceptance
or database migration is performed by these tests.

## Development history

The initial independent-membership implementation and the combined-search
follow-up were tested on 2026-10-29. Their intermediate test totals and file
lists are superseded by the final validation record and complete file list
below. The existing child-edit regressions were retained throughout.

Early runs identified a search-engine viewer accessor error and empty SQL
hierarchy filters after removing hidden parents. Those implementation defects
were fixed. Test fixture issues and class-map ordering failures were also fixed
before the final successful runs. These historical failures are not outstanding
regressions.

## Combined-search follow-up (2026-10-29)

Before changing the datasource, three new isolated regression cases failed:
Web Maniphest search, `maniphest.search`, and anonymous public-child search
returned a task for G/P/C despite P being hidden. The existing 60 tests passed.
After fixing that path, an additional slug regression reproduced the Web
parser silently dropping P's hidden slug. Both defects are now addressed.

Four new test methods cover:

| Input / viewer | Expected behavior |
| --- | --- |
| Bob: C, G/C, duplicate visible conditions | Visible child task returned; private task excluded |
| Alice: G/P/C | Both fixture child tasks returned under their own policies |
| Bob: all six G/P/C orders and duplicate hidden conditions | No results |
| Bob: hidden P alone, G/P, unrelated hidden project or nonexistent PHID | No results |
| Hidden conditions mixed with `any()`, `not()` and `only()` | No results |
| Direct ancestor datasource reused for Bob/Alice/Bob | Original conditions rechecked for each viewer |
| Visible slug and uppercase slug mixed with PHIDs | Normal visible results and deduplication |
| Hidden/unrelated/missing slugs alone or mixed with G/C | No results in Web and Conduit searches |
| Anonymous viewer with public G and C, private P | C and its public task accessible; P hidden |
| Anonymous public C with private P | No edit/join right or synthetic membership; C milestone visible, P milestone hidden |
| Public access disabled in scoped test configuration | C, its task and the application unavailable anonymously |

The Web tests exercise request parsing, saved-query conversion, project-search
extensions and the policy-aware task query. They also render full task-search
pages and verify that hidden project names/links and private task titles do
not appear. Conduit cases invoke the real `maniphest.search` method. Anonymous
tests use native public policies, not an authenticated or omnipotent viewer.
The anonymous request fixture supplies its normal CSRF context; when public
access is disabled, tests check the application gate instead of invoking a
datasource which is unavailable to that viewer.

## Final validation record (2026-10-30)

The candidate contains **26 files: 22 tracked modifications and four new files**.
Of these, 24 are PHP files and two are documentation files. The old 16 tracked /
17 total count preceded the two search fixes; the subsequent 18 tracked /
19 total count preceded the handoff implementation. Neither is the current total.

| Check | Final result | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Core project, handoff and policy regressions | 82/82 passed | `handoff-core8.log` |
| Extended project and policy suite | 97/97 passed, including core tests | `handoff-extended3.log` |
| HTTP login, handoff and rejection checks | 69/69 passed | `handoff-http-final2.json` |
| Baseline HTTP, hidden ancestors and combined search | 83/83 passed | `http-verification.json` |
| PHP syntax | All 24 changed/new PHP files passed | `handoff-php-lint.log`; repeated during pre-commit review |
| Whitespace/error checks | `git diff --check` passed | Pre-commit review |
| Isolated browser acceptance | User confirmed completed and behavior as expected | User acceptance supplied after the automated report |

The core and extended counts overlap and must not be added together. The
extended suite includes library-map, method visibility, Conduit, Celerity and
translation checks. These results are for the accepted local candidate on
PHP 8.3, not an untested integration with a later upstream revision.

The logs and JSON reports are retained outside this repository in the isolated
lab's `browser-review/reports/` directory. The older machine-generated summary
still says browser acceptance is pending; the later user confirmation is the
source for the browser result above. No screenshot or per-step browser trace
is claimed. Reports, test credentials, backups and deployment configuration are
not part of this change.

Before this documentation update, all 26 candidate file hashes matched the
accepted snapshot, and 4,958 source files matched the tested image manifest.
Only this document is updated during submission preparation; the accepted PHP
implementation and tests remain unchanged. The read-only pre-commit review
rechecked PHP syntax and evidence without rerunning database-writing tests.

The core command is shown above. The final extended command, run only in a
writable disposable source copy against isolated storage, was:

```sh
php bin/unit --no-coverage \
  src/applications/project/ \
  src/applications/policy/
```

The full `.arclint` run remains unavailable without the `arc` executable.
PHP 8.4/8.5 and the complete paired-runtime CI were not run in this validation.
No known failure remains in the final regression suites listed above.

Code review readiness: **PASS** for the accepted local candidate. Integration
with the current upstream base requires its own compatibility checks and CI.
This does not certify arbitrary plugin consumers or production data migration.

Production deployment readiness: **BLOCKED pending the separate historical
data, restored-data simulation and deployment acceptance gates** described
above. Local browser acceptance does not approve production member lists or a
production cutover. Do not auto-convert legacy membership edges or reindex
production as part of this change. Large real-world search and handoff workloads
have not been performance-tested. Saved searches containing unavailable or
invalid tags may now return no results and should be corrected by their owners.

## Upstream compatibility check (2026-10-30)

The candidate was applied in a separate checkout to upstream
`b0edc13049e562c9d5e7e25e6e848bcc07d1ec40`, 36 commits ahead of the original
base. The 17 upstream-changed files overlap this candidate only in the Chinese
translation file, at different locations. Patch validation and application
passed without conflict or source edits. The development branch was not merged
or rebased, and the accepted browser installation was not updated.

On a new internal network with independent MySQL and Gorge file storage,
the combined tree passed **82/82 core tests** and **105/105 extended tests**.
The latter includes the original 97-test suite, three Typeahead/search-engine
tests and five upstream Conduit call/controller tests. All **30 PHP files**
changed by either side passed syntax checks. The upstream PHP security check
passed on PHP 8.3.36, and both checkouts passed `git diff --check`.

Initial runs exposed missing test-environment dependencies: Gorge file storage,
the public-policy fixture setting, and prepared XHPAST/PHP-Parser artifacts.
Those were supplied only in the temporary environment; no application source
or test assertions were changed. The failed logs were retained alongside the
final passing logs. Production and the accepted browser environment were not
used as test backends.

These are automated compatibility results. The earlier 69/83 HTTP checks and
user browser acceptance still refer to the original-base candidate. A later
rebase must match this combined tree; changes require renewed regression
testing. Review the updated workboard UI and handoff flows in a separate
browser environment before release. PHP 8.4/8.5 and complete upstream CI remain
separate validation gates.

